= Attention =

== Overview ==
'''Attention''' is selective prioritization of information, locations, features, objects, tasks, or internal representations for enhanced processing or control.

Because processing capacity and goals are limited, organisms do not process all available information equally.

== Selective attention ==
Selective attention favors some information over competing information.

Examples include attending to one conversation in a noisy room, searching for a visual target, tracking one location, focusing on an internal memory, or switching between tasks.

Selection can be influenced by goals and by stimulus properties.

== Attention and awareness ==
Attention and conscious awareness are related but not identical.

People can attend without later reporting every detail, and some stimuli can influence processing despite limited conscious awareness.

== Salience and goals ==
[[Salience]] can make a stimulus stand out because of contrast, novelty, value, or learned significance.

Goal-directed attention can favor information even when it is not physically salient.

== Limited resources ==
Attention is often described as limited, but "resource" is a theoretical metaphor rather than one measurable fluid.

Interference depends on whether tasks compete for common representations, control processes, sensory channels, or response mechanisms.

== Attention and memory ==
[[Working memory]] helps maintain goals and task-relevant information.

Attention influences what is encoded into [[Memory]], while memories and expectations guide what is attended.

